BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Secret Song Club presents: El Tee\, Justice Ramos-Martin\, Frances
 ca Lee
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260725T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260725T193000
UID:260714ZQlqp3oABEmHCr1eQCCUGQ
DESCRIPTION:$15 General Admission\n\nDoors at 7:30pm.\n\nShow from 8:15-10
 :30pm with one intermission\n\nEarlybirds!\n\n$3 off each ticket when you 
 use the code earlybird3 at checkout. Offer ends July 19th.\n\nPromo Co
 des are not applicable at the door.\n\nCodes are not case sensitive\, bu
 t must be typed as one word.\n\nCalifornian-Melbournian artist El Tee ha
 s been building a devoted following in the indie scene since 2018. The ind
 ie-folk\, alt-rock artist writes songs about grief and love and loss\, jux
 taposing her dark-mood lyrics with unassuming softness and much welcomed i
 ntimacy. In 2025\, El Tee released her EP\, ‘Kind Safe Cool’\, accompa
 nied by her second Australian national headline tour\, solidifying her rep
 utation as a compelling live performer.\n\nEl Tee released her debut album
 \, 'Everything Is Fine'\, in 2020 to critical acclaim\, earning her a shor
 tlist nod for Best Rock Act at the Music Victoria Awards and a sold-out al
 bum launch. Her music has topped Australian community radio charts\, garne
 red spins on triple J and US college radio\, and landed rotation on KEXP. 
 With a growing international presence and a new body of work in progress\,
  El Tee is poised to bring her intimate\, emotionally resonant sound to gl
 obal audiences.\n\nEl Tee has been compared to the likes of Angel Olsen an
 d Sharon Van Etten. Often backed by a four-piece band\, she has taken her 
 songs across Australia\, California\, and Europe\, sharing line-ups and fe
 stival stages with acts such as Julia Jacklin \\(US\\)\, Blake Scott \\(Au
 s\\)\, Good Morning \\(Aus\\)\, and Ryan Downey \\(Aus\\)\, steadily gathe
 ring a devoted following along the way\n\n@elteemusic Spotify: El Tee\n\nJ
 ustice Ramos-Martin is a Mexican-American singer-songwriter built on the 
  mountains and oceans of Northern California\, the stormy hills of Arkans
 as\, and the highways in between. Tastefully indoctrinated since birth by 
 a family vinyl collection\, he’s invented a sense of self and artistry t
 hrough unique guitar playing\, and honest lyrics. He writes with a pen on 
 paper\, for a human experience. Both for the listener\, and himself. His i
 nfluences include Paul Simon\, Joni Mitchell\, Jeff Buckley\, Elliot Smith
 \, The Sundays\, Gregory Alan Isakov\, and many more. In October 2025\, he
  released his debut EP Born Into a Fortune.\n\n@justiceramosmartin Spotify
 : Justice Ramos Martin\n\nFrancesca Lee is an SF-based singer-songwriter 
 whose music blends intimate storytelling\, warm expressive vocals\, and ti
 meless melodies. Her songs explore love\, memory\, and everyday detail wit
 h quiet emotional depth\, creating a grounded and personal sound rooted in
  observation and feeling\, and a distinctive voice in the contemporary sin
 ger-songwriter tradition.\n\nfrancesceleemusic.com @francescaleemusic Spot
 ify: Francesca Lee\n\nSecret Song Club is an event production organization
  that presents music parties\, songwriter showcases\, and open mics in bac
 kyards\, wineries\, and alternative venues around Sonoma County. We’re a
 ll about supporting emerging artists and creating listening spaces where p
 eople can hear music with less background chatter. Learn more at InsideLa
 nds.org https://www.instagram.com/secretsongclub
LOCATION:The Lost Church
URL:https://thelostchurch.my.salesforce-sites.com/ticket#/instances/a0FUh0
 0000AAsMLMA1
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cherry Poppin' Daddies
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260725T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260725T000000
UID:260714YMpaUJCYxkuoUH7NltW1DA
DESCRIPTION:In the past few years Cherry Poppin’ Daddies have become the
  kind of band that you would want to book if you were throwing a soiree at
  the Gatsby mansion. Penning elegant\, lush\, pitch perfect arrangements o
 f much of the great American songbook\, from Cole Porter to Frank Sinatra\
 , the Daddies have surprised many- likely familiar with them from the earl
 y 90’s- whose limited exposure to the band undoubtedly consists of an im
 age of spray painted vans careening across the US as an   iconoclastic\, m
 isfit part of an already marginalized punk/ska scene.\n\n   \n\nIn fact th
 e Daddies have of late put together a number of excellent swing/ jazz sit 
 down theatre type shows\, each tailored to a different era of swing music:
  a “Jazz Age Lawn Party” consisting of roaring 20’s and Cotton Club 
 Era material\, a swanky “Live at the Sands” type tribute to the Rat Pa
 ck\, and a World War 2 “Christmas Canteen” show that is part Tiki\, pa
 rt Benny Goodman and comes across like a radio broadcast from Guadalcanal 
 circa 1942. The sheer craftsmanship and professional attention to detail o
 f these performances have shed new light on this criminally underestimated
  band. \n\n   \n\nOn top of being a show band nonpareil\, the Daddies have
  an “oh by the way” double platinum ability to pen original material t
 hat\, while influenced by a history of American popular music\, is wholly 
 modern lyrically\, and itself stands up as classic. The 1997 release of Zo
 ot Suit Riot set the bar for the neo-swing movement of the era in terms of
  originality\, lyrical depth\, and flat out musical sales. \n\n  \n\nWhen 
 you see the Daddies perform their original material you are struck by the 
 poetry and audacity of artistic vision. Whole stirring narratives are laid
  down in swinging couplets that evince dual meaning and social critique. C
 onsider Master and Slave from 1990…\n\n“Friends and Romans I’m your 
 brother\n\nI’m scratchin’ to hang on\n\nThe pursuit of happiness is ju
 st a carpetbaggers con\n\nWhen a can of pork and beans could change my att
 itude\n\nYou wont give it up\, but I could’ve been you\n\nWhile the mast
 ers and the slaves scratch for pieces of the dream\n\nFor purple mountains
  majesty whatever the hell that means\n\nThey give up on each other and th
 at’s the way they get ahead\n\nBut I can still see the stars\n\nThrough 
 these red\, white and blue prison bars”\n\n…ok\, this is not Cole Port
 er clever\, but it’s not trying to be\, it’s something striving to be 
 new\, to be challenging\, courageous\, and truthful. It’s not art simply
  designed to match the couch. What kind of band takes these kind of chance
 s\, both musically and thematically? The Daddies\, that’s who.  \n\nSing
 er songwriter/ bandleader Steve Perry puts it this way\, “Lately\, by la
 ying our hands on the great American songbook\, we have been trying to fil
 l in for audiences the historical back story of what inspires our original
  brand of Americana\, because in essence that is what I consider us to be\
 , an Americana band. But instead of taking mostly from a delta blues tradi
 tion- like many of the bands labeled “Americana”- we adhere mostly to 
 a pre rock\, pre mid 50’s\, hot jazz and essentially Tin Pan Alley tradi
 tion. Then we attempt to deconstruct and détourne those set of tropes to 
 fit modern themes\, be that something like child abuse or say the opioid c
 risis. It’s unfortunate\, but I believe it comforts modern audiences to 
 see that we are able to play\, in an orthodox fashion\, material that is i
 n the tradition\, before they allow us to reach out for something unfamili
 ar\, multifaceted\, or god forbid anti-bourgeois in point of view like man
 y of our originals are”\n\nSadly\, over the past year the band has been 
 laying low and playing a bit closer to home in order that Perry can be a c
 aregiver to his wife\, business partner\, and video muse\, Yvette “Cherr
 y” Perry who was recently diagnosed with stage IV colorectal cancer. “
 This setback for our family has brought us closer to our fans\, who have b
 een so giving of their best wishes and helping with small donations to Yve
 tte’s cancer care. We pledge to continue our advocacy work nationally to
 ward earlier colorectal screening protocols and baseline cancer research s
 o that this scourge that has been loosed on so many families like ours can
  be wiped out sooner rather than later.” \n\nProximity to home has allow
 ed the Eugene\, Oregon band to recently finish a new recording slated for 
 release in late 2018 called Bigger Life. Perry says\, “Bigger Life is 14
  songs that will allow us to touch base with our punk/ ska audience. We wi
 ll continue to play the all swing\, classy theater type shows that are our
  bread and butter\, but in addition\, at some point in 2019 and 2020\, we 
 would like to book a few special tours and play occasional festivals where
  we crank it up and play only ska punk\, hopefully joining forces with som
 e of the bands that befriended us as we were all coming up in this busines
 s. This new record turned out really special and we are excited for people
  to get their ears on it.”
LOCATION:Petaluma Music Festival
URL:https://www.bandsintown.com/e/1038187586?app_id=pkvts0on0m&came_from=2
 87&utm_medium=api&utm_source=partner_api&utm_campaign=event
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
