BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:From Fiery Caldera to Hidden Gem: A Short History of Sugarloaf
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T110000
UID:2607112-QJMnRZw0OL_ihkal4zgA
DESCRIPTION:Join Sugarloaf docents to learn about the history of 
 Sugarloaf Ridge State Park—from its volcanic beginnings 3-5 mill
 ion years ago\, through the indigenous Wappo who tended its fore
 sts\, fields and streams for over 10\,000 years from their villa
 ge of Wilikos at the headwaters of Sonoma Creek\, to homestead 
 settlements and their ingenious adaptations to our isolation and 
 serpentine soils. We will also touch on the park’s natural his
 tory which intersects its human history in often interesting ways
 .\n\nMeet outside the Visitor Center. Tickets are $10 for general
  audiences\, $5 for students\, youth \\(12-17 year olds must be
  accompanied by a parent or legal guardian\\)\, Sugarloaf  membe
 rs\, Sugarloaf or RFO volunteers\, and free for children aged 11
  and 10. This event is not for children under 10 years old. A
 ll children under 18 must be accompanied by a parent or legal 
 guardian. Tickets day-of on site are $15 and might not be avail
 able. Wear comfortable clothing\, appropriate footwear for rocky/sl
 ippery trails\, and sun protection. Bring at least a quart of w
 ater\, hiking poles\, and if you desire\, a snack. Parking fees
  apply.
LOCATION:Sugarloaf Ridge State Park
URL:https://sec.regfox.com/caldera
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Extended Run: Who Will Dance with Pancho Villa?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T193000
UID:260707lRkGs4otLk-F8ERyjIJRaQ
DESCRIPTION:*Who Will Dance with Pancho Villa?* played to sold-out audien
 ces in January. First performed off-Broadway in 1994\, the playwrights upd
 ated the story with the introduction of the **Ballet Folklórico Legado d
 e Mi Alegría de Cloverdale Ca** while developing the play further throug
 h the Raven Players’ ScripTease program. It is the story of a young Chic
 ano from Chicago\, lured to the East Coast by an Ivy League scholarship. A
 s he tries to make a life there\, he neglects family relationships and dee
 p cultural roots established throughout generations. It takes his mother
 ’s nearly fatal heart attack\, help from a Jewish therapist\, and the vi
 sitation of the spirits of his long dead relatives (and of his historical 
 idol\, Pancho Villa) to help him realize what he’s lost\, and how to get
  it back. Dancing spirits\, who are intertwined throughout the play\, help
  remind “Chicano” of the joy in dancing\, especially when dancing is m
 uch more than dancing.\n\nBy John and Gabriel Fraire\n\nDirected by Jenna 
 Vera Dolcini\n\n**August 7\, 8\, 9\, \\*13\, 14\, 15\, 16**\n\n**Friday an
 d Saturday Shows:** 7:30 PM\n\n**Sunday Matinee:** 2:00 PM\n\n**\\*Value 
 Thursday:** 7:30 PM\n\n**Admission: Adults:** $25\n\n**Student w/ID:** $1
 0\n\n**\\*Value Thursday Performance:** Pay What You Can
LOCATION:The Raven Performing Arts Theater
URL:https://raventheater.org/events/who-will-dance-with-pancho-villa-2026/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
